Subject: [PATCH 4/4] winrbd: the BIOS part of the driver (ipxe/src/rbd_bios.c)

Wires the BIOS-only object of the rbd:// driver into a BIOS build, and makes the size of the boot firmware table area visible to
it. Applies after patches 0001 to 0003. Nothing else of iPXE changes, and no EFI build is affected (the object is required on
pcbios only).

 - src/config/config.c: REQUIRE_OBJECT ( rbd_bios ) under SANBOOT_PROTO_RBD and PLATFORM_pcbios. The object holds the
   "rbd-xbft" command (where are the boot firmware tables?) and a shutdown hook that warns when iPXE exits with an RBD drive
   hooked over the PXE ROM's own network stack. Nothing references it otherwise, so without this line it would not be linked.
 - src/include/ipxe/errfile.h: ERRFILE_rbd_bios, the file identifier of that object (iPXE derives the file part of an error
   code from ERRFILE_<object>; the command returns -ENOENT when it finds no table).
 - src/arch/x86/include/int13.h, src/arch/x86/interface/pcbios/int13.c: XBFTAB_SIZE and XBFTAB_ALIGN move from int13.c to
   int13.h, values unchanged (2048, as set by patch 0002, and 16). Why: all boot firmware tables of all SAN devices share ONE
   area of data16 of that size, and int13_install() fails with -ENOSPC (and sanhook / sanboot with it) for a table that does
   not fit. The driver needs room for the largest handoff table (1,024 bytes) next to an iBFT; rbd_bios.c states the number it
   needs and refuses to compile when XBFTAB_SIZE is smaller, so that dropping or weakening patch 0002 is a build error and not
   a boot failure on a machine that also uses iSCSI. The move is the smallest change that lets another object see the constants.

Measured (harness/lab/p2/results/bios2): the handoff table of the live rows is 218 to 248 bytes, the area is 2,048 bytes
(link map: xbftab), the reference iBFT (one target, the longest IQNs) is 512 bytes of fixed blocks plus 512 bytes of strings,
so the largest handoff table next to it fills the area exactly (2,048 of 2,048); a typical iBFT (about 620 bytes) next to a typical
handoff table (240) uses 864.

